The entries of an account
19-the-master-files
Three kinds, which K switches between. The VOC entries of the account under the cursor in Accounts are listed in name order, each with its type (V verb, K keyword, F file, Q pointer to a file in another account, R pointer to a record, PA or PH paragraph, S stored sentence, X text) and what it points at. The dictionary items are those of every file the VOC points at, by the dictionary path of the file pointer, each with its file, id, type (D, I, E, L, PH, X) and column heading. The programs are the records of the account's program files (a directory file named bp, or ending .bp, or with a .out file beside it), each with Built: yes when the compiled program is in the .out file under the same name, - when it is not. The box under the lists shows the entry under the cursor field by field.
A > in the second column means the target lies outside this account: in SDSYS (the shipped pointers lead to @SDSYS/voc.dic and the like) or in another account. For a VOC entry the pointer itself is the account's own record and is edited like any other; what it points at is never edited from this page. A dictionary item marked > lives in another account's dictionary and is only read through this account's pointer; it is edited in the account it lives in. The same holds for the programs of a program file that lies outside the account.
/ keeps what holds a part you type, in the id, and in a dictionary also in the heading. F does the same in every account: it reads them all, so it is run only when you ask, and the box then lists the hits with their accounts. Enter on a hit goes to that entry in its account. Esc takes the find and the filter off. F5 reads again.
E edits the record under the cursor in the editor, in the account it belongs to, and N makes a new one (its id is asked first): VOC entries only in the VOC view, dictionary items only in the dictionary view, as the item form. A pointer record is edited like any other; an item of a dictionary that lies outside the account is refused with the reason, and nothing is written. D deletes the record under the cursor, in the same account and under the same rule, after you type its id in the box (Cancel is the default); a record someone else holds open is not deleted. In the programs view E edits the program and N makes a new one in the editor, in its own account; C compiles it in that account (run.in), and so do the editor's Compile and Catalog; the editor does not run it from here. B is not built on Windows: run.in does not debug in another account, so B and the editor's Debug of such a tab say so; debug the program in its own account. run.in refuses a compile when a word of the command is not the VOC entry SD supplies in that account, when a file it writes lies outside the account, when a file it opens has a trigger, or when the source has $EXECUTE.
D on a program deletes it, after its id is typed, with its compiled program in the .out file (the one the VOC names, else the one beside the program file); a .out file that lies outside the account refuses the delete. A catalog entry of the program is left: it is the account's, and DELETE.CATALOG in that account removes it. Breakpoints marked in the editor's gutter are handed to the debugger the editor starts in the account. Each edit writes one master edit line to the audit trail, each delete one master delete line.
G makes a global change of every row shown: a filter (/) or a find (F) must be on, and a list cut short is refused. It asks for the field (its number, or heading for a dictionary item) and S to set it to a value or R to replace a text in it (field 1, the type of the record, only when one record is shown, and the box then warns that its type changes); for programs it replaces a text in the source and compiles each program changed again in its own account (run.in). One box then shows each account with how many records would change, the first ids, and what would be refused: a record whose file lies outside its account (the pointer rule), or that has no heading. Run (no name is typed) writes each record in its own account; a record another session holds, or one changed since the box, is refused, and a refusal or failure in one account does not stop the others. A box then shows what was changed, refused and failed in each account.
U puts the last global change back. The records as they were are kept in &entries.undo&, a file of SDSYS made the first time (a box shows the create.file command). A record is put back only if it is still exactly what the change wrote; one changed since is left and counted. U is offered until the change has been put back. Each account writes a master global line, and an undo an entries undo line.
